+- [Should-fix] §Ring caps and drain policy point 3 ("WINDOW = 256 KiB ... restores today's small-pipe backpressure ... The client writer blocks the same way it blocks on a slow ssh pipe today"), point 4 (cites the client writer's 5 s write deadline, writer.rs:15); Acceptance "On a healthy but slow live leg ... A 16 MiB clipboard image sent while the leg is up completes without ending the session" — The fixed 256 KiB leg-up window caps throughput at about WINDOW/RTT, so with the client writer's hard 5 s per-frame deadline a 16 MiB ClipboardImage over a healthy leg with RTT above about 80 ms times out and kills the endpoint, contradicting the 16 MiB acceptance criterion and the claim of parity with today's ssh pipe (2 MiB channel window). (new; introduced by the cycle-1 flow-control window that fixed the iteration-1 coalescing finding)
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/client/endpoint/writer.rs write_frame sets `deadline = Instant::now() + WRITE_TIMEOUT` (5 s) once per frame; the reset on progress is `#[cfg(windows)]` only, so on Unix the whole frame must enter the socket within 5 s or the writer returns TimedOut "endpoint write timed out" and the endpoint transport stops. Every client message, ClipboardImage included (clipboard_images.rs:35-41, up to 16 MiB), goes through this writer (client/transport.rs:14, supervisor.rs:530). Spec point 3 reads the client only while unsent+unacked < 256 KiB; acks follow delivery (rule 3), so sustained rate <= 256 KiB/RTT. 16 MiB in 5 s needs about 3.4 MiB/s, so RTT <= ~78 ms; at 100 ms the frame takes ~6.4 s, at 150 ms ~9.6 s. External, fetched by all three seats: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openssh/openssh-portable/master/channels.h defines `CHAN_SES_PACKET_DEFAULT (32*1024)`, `CHAN_SES_WINDOW_DEFAULT (64*CHAN_SES_PACKET_DEFAULT)` (2 MiB) and `CHAN_INPUT_MAX (16*1024*1024)`, so today's plain bridge tolerates roughly 8x the RTT for the same frame. The acceptance bullet (line 327) states no RTT bound; the Flow control decision (line 268) says only "keeps today's small-pipe backpressure". The planned e2e tests use a local fake ssh at near-zero RTT, so CI would pass while WAN links fail. Not Blocking: the failure is a lost paste plus a supervisor reconnect on high-RTT links, not data corruption.
+  fix-shape hint: size WINDOW at or above the ssh channel window (2 MiB) or from a bandwidth-delay estimate, or scope the 16 MiB criterion by RTT; add an e2e case with injected delay (netem or a sleeping relay) that compares the fork against the plain bridge.
