+- [Should-fix] §Session wire protocol Rule 6 "Ordering" ("the leg writer sends a pending ACK, HEARTBEAT or END before the next DATA chunk") vs §Keeper "Server EOF" ("forwards the remaining ring bytes, then END{"server-eof"}, in stream order") and the Per-HELLO *eof* cell; acceptance "A live handoff during a session delivers ServerShutdown ... also holds when the server EOF arrives while the leg is down" — Rule 6's jump-ahead rule names END with no exception, so a writer following it literally sends END{"server-eof"} ahead of the not-yet-sent ring tail; the local side then discards the session (END row: "both sides discard it") before the handoff ServerShutdown frame arrives, breaking the live-handoff acceptance criterion. (new; the rule 6 "Ordering" text is a cycle-1 amendment)
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived (refute seat REJECT, FYI) / ground ✓)
+  evidence: Rule 6 (line 99) names END among the messages sent "before the next DATA chunk"; §Ring caps point 1 repeats "Control messages go ahead of the next chunk (rule 6)". Server EOF (line 220), the eof-state cells (lines 209, 214) and the test expectation at line 442 all require END after the remaining bytes. Point 3 (line 111) limits replay to the 256 KiB window and point 4 (line 110) lets the keeper hold up to 16 MiB while parked, so after EOF the keeper can have unsent DATA queued while END is already pending. The END row (line 85) discards the session on receipt. Acceptance line 321 requires ServerShutdown rather than ConnectionLost, including when EOF arrives while the leg is down and the session resumes, which is where the tail is largest. The refute seat's dissent (the specific "in stream order" text governs the general rule) is acknowledged by both confirming seats; the ground seat calls it an ambiguity rather than a hard contradiction. The majority holds because rule 6's wording explicitly lists END and the other END uses (refusal before WELCOME, ring-overflow END on the next leg) are prompt control messages, so an implementer reading rule 6 as the general case is reasonable. The same reading lets a client-EOF END overtake the client's last upstream DATA. Relation to the iteration-1 spot-rejected nit "END can overtake the client's final upstream bytes": that rejection rested on "DATA and END share an ordered stream"; the amended rule 6 text is new evidence that overrides that ordering, and this finding concerns the server-eof path, so it is not a re-litigation.
+  fix-shape hint: exempt session-final END from the jump-ahead rule (only ACK, HEARTBEAT and the pre-WELCOME refusal END go ahead of DATA), or state that END is queued only once the ring is fully sent.
