+- [Nit] Session wire protocol rule 6 "Silence" ("The clock starts when the local end writes HELLO (the leg's ssh and remote command are up, because the ready marker was seen)") and "Leg setup" ("Leg 0 has no fork setup deadline ... Its remote bring-up is bounded by the attacher's 5 s keeper wait"); Upstream touch list host.rs "after l.24" dispatch hook; §Keeper Rendezvous 5 s waits — The 8 s HELLO-to-WELCOME clock starts at the ready marker, which the shell prints before herdr starts, so it must also cover `ensure_remote_server_running` (up to 5 s server spawn wait) plus the 5 s keeper wait; the stated leg-0 bound is incomplete, and a cold remote on a slow host silently loses resume on leg 0. (new; the "Leg setup" text is the cycle-1 fix for the iteration-1 Nit on the undefined pre-WELCOME deadline, and this finding is about the bound that fix states, not the prior ambiguity)
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived (refute seat REJECT, FYI) / ground ✓)
+  evidence: `posix_remote_output_command` (src/remote/attach.rs:555-557) is `printf '\n%s\n' '<marker>'` followed by the command, so the marker precedes `exec herdr`. The spec's dispatch hook sits "after l.24" of host.rs (spec line 243); host.rs:22 `ensure_remote_server_running()?` runs before that point and on a cold host calls `spawn_server_daemon` then `wait_for_server_socket(.., 5 s)` (host.rs:40-61). The attacher then waits up to 5 s for the keeper socket (spec lines 198-199). The worst case of the stated waits is about 10 s plus process start, against an 8 s silence verdict with no inbound byte before WELCOME. Line 185 maps that to the plain fallback (outcome (c), line 290). Why Nit, per two seats: typical server and keeper spawns take well under 1 s; the leg-0 outcome is the spec's accepted graceful fallback and the next connection retries resume with the server now up; a reconnect leg almost never runs ensure cold, because a live keeper already holds the server connection, so the reconnect-leg Fatal variant is not realistic. The refute seat's REJECT argues the same materiality points and adds that an ensure timeout fails the plain bridge identically; the confirming seats agree on impact and confirm only that the line-100 bound is mis-stated.
+  fix-shape hint: correct the line-100 sentence to include the server-spawn wait and herdr startup; optionally give the pre-WELCOME phase its own deadline (sum of the remote bring-up bounds) on leg 0, or start the clock at the attacher's first byte.
