+- [Nit] §Keeper "Idle exit" ("An attacher therefore sees one of two things: a listening keeper, or a missing socket together with a free live lock") vs §Keeper "Rendezvous" ("The attacher first validates the directory and tries to connect. On ENOENT or ECONNREFUSED it takes the spawn lock") — The lock-ordered idle exit does not deliver its stated guarantee because the attacher's first connect takes no lock, so a connect landing between the keeper's non-blocking backlog drain and listener close still meets a closing keeper. (regressed, fixed incompletely, see iteration 1 Nit "Keeper idle exit has no defined ordering with the rendezvous locks": the cycle-1 fix added the `.spawn` hold and backlog drain, and this is the "Window A" that fix claims to close)
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived (refute seat REJECT, FYI) / ground ✓)
+  evidence: Rendezvous (line 197-198): the first connect precedes the spawn lock, so holding `.spawn` does not exclude it. Idle exit (line 222): the keeper, holding `.spawn`, "accepts any connection already in the listen backlog (non-blocking)" and otherwise "closes the listener, unlinks the socket and exits". A unix-stream connect() succeeds once queued in the backlog, so a connect after the empty accept and before close(listener) succeeds, passes the SO_PEERCRED check (line 196), forwards HELLO and meets EOF or reset. That is a third outcome, so the "one of two things" sentence is false, and the unit test "idle exit against a concurrent attacher (backlogged connect, held spawn lock)" (line 299) cannot cover the final-instant case. Why Nit, all confirming seats: on leg 0, EOF before WELCOME already routes to the plain fallback (lines 168, 185, 290(c)); a reconnect leg cannot meet an idle-exiting keeper because a parked session keeps the table non-empty, and once gone it would get `unknown` (outcome (b)) anyway; the window is microseconds wide after 30 s idle. The refute seat's REJECT agrees on the mechanics and argues the lock still closes the real (spawn-side) window; the confirming seats do not dispute that, only the overstated invariant.
+  fix-shape hint: either take `.spawn` before the first connect (actually closes the race), or drop the backlog-accept step and reword the invariant to say the residual race lands on the leg-0 fallback; adjust the unit-test wording to the cases it can assert.
