+- [FYI] §Ring caps and drain policy points 3 and 4; rule 6 "Silence" (8 s); Acceptance "A saved-machine outage longer than 10 s ... does not trigger the endpoint health timeout" — During the undetected-stall window (up to 8 s before the dead verdict) the 256 KiB leg-up window stops the bridge reading the client, so a bulk client frame can hit the client writer's unresettable 5 s per-frame deadline and tear down the transport before leg-down buffering begins. Rejected 3/3. The mechanism is real (writer.rs:223 sets a hard 5 s per-frame deadline on Unix; point 3 stops source reads at WINDOW while the leg counts as up for 0-8 s; point 4's deadline protection starts only at the dead verdict), but it reaches no stated requirement: point 3 says in plain terms that the client writer "blocks the same way it blocks on a slow ssh pipe today", and a bulk frame meeting a stalled pipe hits the same 5 s deadline today; point 4's "keeps the 5 s deadline from firing" is scoped to "while no leg is up"; point 4 already accepts that a bulk client message written across an outage ends the session (outcome (b), the 16 MiB ClipboardImage example); the exposure needs a single frame larger than roughly 256 KiB plus the socket buffer that overlaps the first ~3 s of a stall, so keystrokes and ordinary input (Problem bullet 3) are unaffected; the health-timeout acceptance criterion (line 316) is a small-traffic case. At most a wording imprecision in point 4's justification. The healthy-leg throughput consequence of the same window is confirmed separately above (16 MiB clipboard finding).
+- [FYI] §Ring caps and drain policy point 3 ("WINDOW = 256 KiB ... This restores today's small-pipe backpressure"); Decision "Flow control" ("keeps today's small-pipe backpressure") — The fixed window caps per-direction throughput at about WINDOW/RTT, roughly 8x below the 2 MiB OpenSSH channel window, so the parity phrase is inaccurate and the throughput regression on long-RTT links is undocumented. Rejected 2/3 (ground seat CONFIRM Nit; it fetched channels.h and confirms the 2 MiB figure). The spec never promises throughput parity: "small-pipe backpressure" describes the server's blocked socket write and render coalescing (line 268 ties it to that purpose), WINDOW is stated as "a fixed compile-time constant, not adaptive pacing" (line 111), adaptive pacing is explicitly deferred to Phases 2-4, and no requirement, non-goal or acceptance criterion sets a throughput or high-RTT target; large transfers are only required to complete, which they do; bounded receive memory (rule 2) is a stated reason for a small fixed window. What is material about the cap (the 5 s writer deadline making the 16 MiB acceptance criterion unachievable above ~80 ms RTT) is carried by the confirmed finding above; the standalone "undocumented tradeoff" claim is a one-line wording note ("semantics", not throughput) for the Flow control decision. The finding's spike (netem at 100-200 ms, fork vs plain bridge) remains a reasonable measurement to run alongside the confirmed fix.
